<%= turbo_frame_tag "feed-page-#{page}", target: "_top" do %>
  <%= render partial: "painting", collection: paintings, locals: { kept_ids: kept_ids } %>

  <% if next_page %>
    <%# Story 0022: the filter params ride every lazy page fetch, or a
        scroll past page 1 of a filtered gallery would quietly drop the
        filter.

        `target: "_top"` HERE, not only on the loaded content's own wrapper
        above: Turbo swaps a frame's fetched response into its *existing*
        DOM element by replacing children, not by replacing the element
        itself — the element's own attributes, set at placeholder time,
        never get overwritten by whatever the response's matching frame tag
        carries. Without this, every artist-name link on page 2+ scopes its
        click to this frame (Turbo's default when no ancestor sets `_top`),
        `/artists/:slug` has no `feed-page-N` frame in its response, and
        Turbo replaces the whole page's worth of cards with the words
        "Content missing" — reported live, reproduced by forcing this
        frame's load and clicking an artist link inside it. %>
    <%= turbo_frame_tag "feed-page-#{next_page}", target: "_top",
          src: feed_path(**filter_params, page: next_page), loading: :lazy do %>
      <div class="sentinel" aria-label="Loading more paintings">
        <span class="sentinel__ring" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="sentinel__label">Fetching more masterpieces</span>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% else %>
    <footer class="coda">
      <div class="ornament" aria-hidden="true">✦</div>
      <%# "Walked the whole gallery" is false under a filter — the reader
          walked a subset (design review pass 2). %>
      <p class="coda__line">
        <%= filtered ? "Every match, end to end." : "You have walked the whole gallery." %>
      </p>
      <%# Names the museums actually represented in the pool, not a fixed list:
          story 0013 draws from four collections and the quota table decides how
          many of each, so a hard-coded sentence would go stale on the next
          reseed. Ordered by weight, so the largest contributor reads first. %>
      <p class="coda__note">
        Every work here is in the public domain, from the collections of
        <%= safe_join(Painting.represented_sources.map { |museum|
              link_to(museum[:name], museum[:url], target: "_blank", rel: "noopener")
            }, ", ") %>.
        Metadata CC0.
      </p>
      <div class="coda__doors">
        <a class="caps-link" href="#top" onclick="window.scrollTo({top: 0, behavior: 'smooth'}); return false;">Back to the beginning ↑</a>
        <%# The end of a filtered walk offered no next step until story 0027
            — a reader who reached the last work in a wing had nowhere to go
            but back up. Unfiltered, the coda already IS the map (the whole
            gallery); a second door to the index would be a door to itself.

            Preserves `filter_params`, same as the rail door (`/code-review`
            caught this dropping them): the index reopens scoped to the
            wing the reader just walked — current marked, co-occurring
            values only — not reset to the whole pool. "Another wing" is
            the door to what's NEXT from here, the same promise the rail
            door already keeps everywhere else on this screen. %>
        <% if filtered %>
          <%= link_to "Another wing", feed_index_path(**filter_params), class: "caps-link" %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </footer>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
